            <ArticleTitle>Antinociceptive effect of intracerebroventricular administration of cholecystokinin antagonist in nerve-ligated mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>169-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In the present study we investigated the effects of intracerebroventricular injection of caerulein, the cholecystoki nin receptor agonist and proglumide, the receptor antagonist on morphine response in the sciatic nerve ligation in mice. Subcutaneous administration of morphine induced antinociception in the both intact and ligated mice, however, the response of the opioid was lower in the ligated mice as compared with the intact animals. Caerulein induced antinociception in the non-ligated but not in the nerve-ligated animals. Combination of caerulein with morphine elicited higher response in ligated animals, however, the response induced in ligated animals was much more prominent. Proglumide alone did not elicit any response in both animal groups. The antagonist decreased the response of caerulein in the nonligated mice. Low doses of proglumide in the combination with caerulein induced antinociception in the ligated mice. We conclude that cholecystokinin receptor mechanism(s) may alter morphine resistance induced by nerve ligation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
